from ../structure import StructuralContext
from ../LanguageData import ContainerProperties, Pronouns
from ../model/OpenItems import Ambiguity, Gap

export anchor Resolution:
    description:
        A statement talks about things by name: the button, its action, it.
        Resolution is working out which things in the specification those
        names mean. Until a name is resolved, a statement cannot become a
        fact, because a fact has to be about something definite.

    whatGetsResolved:
        Four kinds of words and groups need resolving: noun groups such as
        the disabled button, pronouns such as it, possessives such as its
        action, and Piton references written into the prose.

    order:
        Resolution tries five sources in a fixed order and stops at the
        first one that gives an answer. Earlier sources are more reliable, so
        a later source is never consulted once an earlier one has answered.

        ```text
        1  explicit Piton references      @{Button}, ${Button}, {Button}
        2  the statement's structural scope   the entity, its parts, the property path
        3  every other known name          entities and parts anywhere in the analyzed files
        4  language rules                 pronouns, possessives
        5  nothing                        the name stays unresolved
        ```

        A Piton reference in prose is resolved by the compiler, so it is as
        certain as structure. The structural scope is described in
        @{StructuralContext}.

    oneAnswerOrNone:
        A source gives an answer only when it finds exactly one candidate.
        When a source finds more than one, resolution stops there with an
        @{Ambiguity} listing every candidate. It does not move on to a later
        source to break the tie, and it never picks one.

    matchingNames:
        Piton names and English words are written differently, so both are
        reduced to the same form before they are compared: split into words
        at capitals, hyphens and underscores, lowercased, and made singular
        using the dictionary. Determiners such as the and a are ignored.

        ```text
        ButtonComponent          button component
        the button components    button component
        submit-label             submit label
        ```

    pronouns:
        A pronoun from @{Pronouns} stands for the subject of the previous
        statement in the same paragraph, when that subject was resolved. In
        the first statement of a paragraph, it stands for the scope's owner:
        the nearest enclosing entity or part, skipping properties that are
        containers, listed in @{ContainerProperties}, or conditions. If the
        previous subject was not resolved, the pronoun is not resolved
        either.

    possessives:
        A possessive such as its action or the button's text names a part of
        something. It is resolved in two steps: the owner first, then the
        part, which is looked up only among the owner's parts and aspects.
        The part is never looked up elsewhere, because its action cannot mean
        an action that belongs to something else.

        When the owner resolves but has no such part, the part is introduced:
        analysis knows its path, Button.action, but nothing defines it. An
        introduced part can be used in facts, and it is always reported as a
        @{Gap}.

    outcomes:
        Every name ends in one of four outcomes.

        ```text
        resolved     it names exactly one defined thing
        introduced   it names a part by path, but nothing defines that part
        ambiguous    more than one thing fits, and all are listed
        unresolved   nothing fits
        ```

        A fact that depends on an ambiguous or unresolved name is partial.

    repeatedPasses:
        Resolution uses facts, and facts come from resolution: whether
        disabled is a state depends on the statement that lists the states.
        So analysis repeats resolution and pattern matching over all
        statements until a pass changes nothing, in file and line order each
        time. Each pass can only add facts, never remove them, so this always
        ends, at the latest after one pass per statement.

    example:
        Resolving the names in the Button's statements gives this.

        ```text
        description/s1  A button    resolved    Button          2, the entity in scope
        description/s2  its         resolved    Button          4, previous subject
        description/s2  its action  introduced  Button.action   4, possessive
        description/s3  The button  resolved    Button          2, the entity in scope
        description/s4  It          resolved    Button          4, previous subject
        disabled/s1     Its         resolved    Button          4, scope owner
        disabled/s1     Its color   resolved    Button.color    4, possessive; color is
                                                                   an aspect from
                                                                   description/s3
        disabled/s2     Its text    introduced  Button.text     4, possessive
        disabled/s3     It          resolved    Button          4, previous subject
        ```
